  "skills/flutter-feature/SKILL.md": "---\nname: flutter-feature\ndescription: Add a new feature, screen, or flow to a Flutter app following the project's existing architecture and state management (Riverpod, Bloc, Provider, GetX, or plain setState). Use when asked to build a screen, feature, page, form, or list.\nlicense: MIT\n---\n\n# Flutter Feature\n\nBuild a feature that looks like the rest of the codebase wrote it.\n\n## 1. Learn the shape\n\n1. Read `pubspec.yaml` (or the project profile in `CLAUDE.md` / `AGENTS.md`) for state management, routing, and codegen.\n2. Open the most similar existing feature. Note its folder layout (`features/x/{data,domain,presentation}` vs `screens/` + `models/`), file names, and how it gets dependencies.\n3. If there is no existing feature to copy, propose a minimal layout in one or two lines and confirm before creating many files.\n\n## 2. Build in this order\n\n1. **Model**: immutable class. Use `freezed` / `json_serializable` only if the project already does.\n2. **Data**: repository or service that matches existing ones. Keep I/O out of widgets.\n3. **State**: follow the detected solution:\n   - **Riverpod**: `Notifier` / `AsyncNotifier`. Use `@riverpod` codegen only if `riverpod_generator` is a dependency. Read with `ref.watch` in build and `ref.read` in callbacks.\n   - **Bloc**: match the project's choice of `Cubit` or `Bloc`. Use sealed classes for states and events if the project does. One bloc per feature, provided where similar blocs are provided.\n   - **Provider**: `ChangeNotifier` plus `ChangeNotifierProvider` placed where siblings are.\n   - **GetX**: controller plus binding, following the existing pattern.\n   - **None**: `StatefulWidget` / `setState`. Don't introduce a library.\n4. **UI**: small widgets, `const` where possible, theme values from `Theme.of(context)` instead of hard-coded colors and sizes. Handle loading, empty, and error states the way existing screens do.\n5. **Navigation**: register the route the same way as others (`go_router` route table, `auto_route` annotations, or `Navigator`).\n6. **Tests**: at least one widget test for the screen's main states, plus unit tests for non-trivial logic. Mock the way existing tests do (`mocktail` / `mockito`).\n\n## 3. Finish\n\n- If codegen is used, run `dart run build_runner build --delete-conflicting-outputs`.\n- Run the `flutter-verify` loop.\n- Report the files you created and changed, and anything you assumed.\n",
  "skills/flutter-guidelines/SKILL.md": "---\nname: flutter-guidelines\ndescription: Behavioral guidelines for writing, reviewing, or refactoring Flutter/Dart code. Use on any Flutter coding task to avoid overcomplication, unrequested dependencies, and noisy diffs, and to finish with a verified, analyzer-clean result.\nlicense: MIT\n---\n\n# Flutter Guidelines\n\nBehavioral guidelines that reduce common LLM mistakes in Flutter projects. Adapted for Flutter from the\n[Karpathy guidelines](https://github.com/forrestchang/andrej-karpathy-skills) (MIT).\n\n**Tradeoff:** these bias toward caution over speed. For trivial tasks, use judgment.\n\n## 1. Think Before Coding\n\n**Read the project before writing to it.**\n\n- Check `pubspec.yaml` first: state management, routing, codegen (`freezed`, `json_serializable`, `riverpod_generator`), and lint rules in `analysis_options.yaml`.\n- Find an existing feature that is similar and follow its structure. The project's conventions beat your defaults.\n- State your assumptions. If the request has more than one reasonable reading (e.g. \"add caching\": memory or disk?), ask instead of picking silently.\n- Never introduce a second state-management or routing solution next to the existing one.\n\n## 2. Simplicity First\n\n**Minimum code that solves the problem.**\n\n- No new dependencies unless the user agrees. Say which package and why before adding it.\n- Prefer a `StatelessWidget` plus existing state over a new controller, service, or base class.\n- No abstractions, interfaces, or generic widgets for code used once.\n- Use `const` constructors where possible; don't add `Key`s, mixins, or theme extensions nobody asked for.\n- If you wrote 200 lines and 50 would do, rewrite it.\n\n## 3. Surgical Changes\n\n**Every changed line traces to the request.**\n\n- Format only the files you touched (`dart format <files>`), not the whole project.\n- Never hand-edit generated files (`*.g.dart`, `*.freezed.dart`, `*.gr.dart`, `*.mocks.dart`). Change the source and run `dart run build_runner build --delete-conflicting-outputs`.\n- Don't bump dependency versions, change `analysis_options.yaml`, or touch platform folders (`android/`, `ios/`, ...) unless the task needs it.\n- Match existing naming, file layout, and import style (relative vs `package:`).\n- Mention unrelated problems you notice; don't fix them. Remove only the imports and code that your own change made unused.\n\n## 4. Goal-Driven Execution\n\n**Define done as something you can check.**\n\n- \"Add a screen\" → it builds, is reachable from the router, and has a widget test.\n- \"Fix the bug\" → write a test that reproduces it, then make it pass.\n- \"Refactor\" → tests pass before and after, and public behavior is unchanged.\n\nDone means `flutter analyze` reports no new issues and the relevant tests pass (see the `flutter-verify` skill).\nNever silence a lint with `// ignore:` to get there unless the user agrees.\n",
  "skills/flutter-verify/SKILL.md": "---\nname: flutter-verify\ndescription: Verification loop for Flutter/Dart changes - format, analyze, codegen, and test, then fix and repeat until clean. Use before declaring any Flutter coding task done, or when asked to check, validate, or fix analyzer errors and failing tests.\nlicense: MIT\n---\n\n# Flutter Verify\n\nDon't call a task done until it passes this loop.\n\n## The loop\n\n1. **Codegen**, only if you changed annotated sources (`@freezed`, `@JsonSerializable`, `@riverpod`, ...):\n   `dart run build_runner build --delete-conflicting-outputs`\n2. **Format** only the files you changed: `dart format <changed files>`\n3. **Analyze**: `flutter analyze` (use `dart analyze` for pure Dart packages).\n   - Fix every issue your change introduced. Leave existing issues alone, but mention them.\n   - Don't add `// ignore:` comments or change `analysis_options.yaml` to get a clean result.\n4. **Test**, narrowest first:\n   - The tests for what you touched: `flutter test test/path/to/feature_test.dart`\n   - Then the full suite: `flutter test`\n5. If anything fails, read the **first** error, fix the cause (not the symptom), and go back to step 2.\n\n## Reading failures\n\n- A `pumpAndSettle` timeout usually means an infinite animation or a progress indicator. Use `pump(duration)` instead.\n- \"No MaterialLocalizations found\" or missing providers: wrap the widget under test the way other tests do (`MaterialApp`, `ProviderScope`, `BlocProvider`).\n- Golden mismatches: never update goldens (`--update-goldens`) unless the visual change was intended and the user agrees.\n- `Undefined name` after editing a model: generated code is stale. Rerun step 1.\n\n## Reporting\n\nSay exactly what ran and what it returned, for example \"`flutter analyze`: no issues. `flutter test`: 42 passed.\" If something still fails, say so with the error. Never claim a check passed if you didn't run it.\n",
  "templates/project_rules.md": "## Flutter agent rules\n\n{{profile}}\n\n### How to work\n- **Think first:** read `pubspec.yaml` and a similar existing feature before writing code. State your assumptions and ask when the request is ambiguous.\n- **Simplicity:** use the minimum code that solves the request. Add no new dependencies, abstractions, or configurability without asking. Never add a second state-management or routing solution.\n- **Surgical changes:** touch only what the task needs. Format only the files you changed. Never hand-edit generated files (`*.g.dart`, `*.freezed.dart`); rerun `build_runner` instead.\n- **Goal-driven:** define done as a check: `flutter analyze` reports no new issues and the relevant tests pass. Write a failing test first for bugs. Don't silence lints with `// ignore:`.\n\n### Verify before finishing\n1. `dart run build_runner build --delete-conflicting-outputs` (only if annotated sources changed)\n2. `dart format <changed files>`\n3. `flutter analyze`\n4. `flutter test <related tests>`, then `flutter test`\n\nReport what ran and its real result.\n",
